  [1mWhere this landed[0m

  You asked for a notifications system. I'm handing you a spec for a [1mtask app[0m instead, and that's a real redirection, so
  here's the reasoning in one place:

  The app is an empty HTML page. Notifications needs four things to exist — a task, a user, a detected [3mchange[0m, and a
  channel. None were present. Writing a notifications spec here would have meant inventing all four and calling the
  invention a design.

  So the work is two cycles, and [1mcycle 1 is built specifically to make cycle 2 cheap[0m. Three hooks carry that: a durable
  identity on every record, an append-only event log so a [3mchange[0m is a first-class thing rather than a diff you
  reconstruct, and an explicit [38;5;153mwatchers[39m relationship for "cares about." Those are the parts that are a rewrite if
  retrofitted, and they're the reason this isn't just "build a todo list first."

  One consequence I want to make sure you saw, since it's the most likely place this disappoints you: [1mbrowser-only means
[0m  [1mnotifications can only fire while the tab is open.[0m No email, no phone buzz, no cross-device. If "notified" in your
  head meant any of those, the persistence decision needs to flip to a server, and now is far cheaper than after cycle 1
  ships. That's a live option — say so and I'll revise.

  Decisions you made: single-user but multi-user-ready · browser-only behind a storage seam · moderate task model · unit
  tests only. Decisions I took on your instruction: the append-only log, the four-module split, and the two-cycle
  ordering.

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-task-app-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it and let me know
  if you want changes before I write the implementation plan.

